## 1 Introduction

Fast Radio Bursts (FRBs) are bright pulses with large dispersion measures (DMs) at radio frequency, with millisecond-duration randomly occurring at cosmological distances (Petroff et al., 2019; Xiao et al., 2021; Zhang, 2022). FRB was first discovered in 2007 (Lorimer et al., 2007), and some FRBs were observed to be active repeatedly (Spitler et al., 2016). Whether there are two intrinsically different categories, repeating one and non-repeating one, has not been confirmed yet. Up to now, over 600 FRBs have been reported (CHIME/FRB Collaboration et al., 2020). However, their physical origins are still poorly known. It has been speculated that the central engines of FRBs are magnetars (Popov and Postnov, 2013; Kulkarni et al., 2014; Murase et al., 2016; Katz, 2016; Metzger et al., 2017; Wang and Yu, 2017; Beloborodov, 2017; Lu and Kumar, 2018; Yang and Zhang, 2018; Wadiasingh and Timokhin, 2019; Wang et al., 2020). The discovery of FRB 20200428 produced by Galactic magnetar SGR J1935+2154 confirmed that at least some FRBs are from magnetars (Bochenek et al., 2020; CHIME/FRB Collaboration et al., 2020).

The variations of the RM and DM can provide us with important information about the surrounding environment of FRBs. Long-term monitoring of repeating FRBs revealed that the majority of them show large RM variations, suggesting them in dynamical magneto-ionic environments (Mckinven et al., 2023). FRB 20121102 has a large RM (\\sim 10^{5} rad m{}^{-2}) (Michilli et al., 2018), which decreased by 30% in about two years (Hilmarsson et al., 2021) and 70% till to 2023 (Feng et al., 2023). It may be caused by the wind nebula of a young magnetar (Margalit and Metzger, 2018; Zhao and Wang, 2021), the ejecta from the progenitors (massive stars or compact binary mergers Piro and Gaensler, 2018; Zhao et al., 2021), or the outflow from an SMBH (Zhang, 2018; Katz, 2020; Yang et al., 2023). FRB 20201124A showed a short-time variation of RM (Xu et al., 2022) and the first local RM reversal (Wang et al., 2022). A magnetar/Be star binary is promising to explain the unusual features of FRB 20201124A (Wang et al., 2022; Lu et al., 2023), including the RM variation and reversal, depolarization and Faraday conversion. FRB 20190520B was found to reside in a dwarf galaxy at redshift z=0.241. It has a lager host DM \\sim 900 pc cm{}^{-3}(Niu et al., 2022), whichmay be related to a young supernova remnant (Zhao and Wang, 2021; Katz, 2022). FRB 20190520B showed a large value of RM and the RM reversal (Anna-Thomas et al., 2023), which can be explained in massive binary models (Wang et al., 2022; Zhao et al., 2023; Anna-Thomas et al., 2023). FRB 20180301A also showed a varying RM with reversal, which also indicates a binary origin (Kumar et al., 2023). However, a binary origin is not necessary to interpret the RM fluctuations. As shown by Beniamini et al. (2022), if the FRB passes through a region with turbulent and even weakly magnetized plasma can result in stochastic RM fluctuations.

Extremely large RMs also have been observed in the vicinity of massive black holes. For example, PSR J1745-2900, which resides 0.12 pc from Sgr A^{*} was estimated to have an \\rm RM=-6.69\\times 10^{4} rad m{}^{-2}(Eatough et al., 2013). The RM variation is about 3500 rad m{}^{-2}(Desvignes et al., 2018). PSR J1746-2850, which is close to Sgr A{}^{*}, is reported to have \\rm RM=-12234 rad m{}^{-2} with variation of 300-400 rad m{}^{-2}(Abbate et al., 2023). So it is possible that some FRB sources may be near SMBHs. Combining with the evidence that one magnetar can result in producing of FRB-like bursts, we consider an FRB-generating magnetar orbiting an SMBH, called EMRI.

EMRI is one of the main sources expected for the future space-borne GW detector LISA to detect (Amaro-Seoane et al., 2017, 2023). When a neutron star (NS) inspirals into an SMBH, the system emits significant amounts of continuous gravitational waves (GWs) along each orbit in the frequency domain to which LISA will be most sensitive. Such GWs can be regarded as'standard siren' if the information about redshift is available. In this case, it is natural to seek the EM counterpart of GW carrying the redshift information. Although it is currently believed that there are no such EM counterparts of EMRI signals in most cases, some models have proposed that EMRI can be associated with EM signals. For example, a new formation channel of EMRIs with tidal disruption flares as EM counterparts was proposed by Wang et al. (2019). Quasi-periodic eruptions generated from the Roche lobe overflows of an evolved star of white dwarf orbiting an SMBH can be detected as EMRI sources with EM counterparts (King, 2020; Zhao et al., 2022). Recently, some indications for the EM counterpart of GWs have been found. For instance, GW 190425, a binary NS merger event, was reported to be associated with a one-off FRB 20190425A with the chance probability 2.8 \\sigma(Moroianu et al., 2023). However, this association was questioned by Bhardwaj et al. (2023). Sridhar et al. (2021) and Katz (2023) proposed that periodic FRBs can be produced from the extreme mass ratio binaries that contain ordinary stars and intermediate mass black holes. In this letter, we propose that FRBs may be the EM counterparts of EMRIs.

This letter is organized as follows. The relative FRB progenitor models are discussed in Section 2. The EMRI signals produced in the NS-SMBH system are given in Section 3. The event rate is estimated in Section 4. Discussions and conclusions are given in Section 5

## 2 FRB progenitor models

Since the discovery of FRB 20200428, progenitors related to magnetars have received increasing attention. Several magnetar progenitor models were proposed, for more details see reviews by Xiao et al. (2021) and Zhang (2020). Here we focus only on the single magnetar model proposed by Kumar and Bosnjak (2020) and the cosmic comb model proposed by Zhang (2017).

### Single magnetar model

The coherent curvature radiation in the magnetosphere may be a possible radiation mechanism of FRBs. In this model, the disturbance near the surface of the magnetar launches an Alfven wave packet, and a fraction of the wave energy is converted into coherent radio radiation. Alfven waves with the non-zero component of transverse wave-vector require an electric current along the magnetic field, which can be supplied by the counter-streaming electron-positron pairs moving at near the speed of light at larger radii as the plasma density decreases with distance from the magnetar surface. The bunch formation is attributed to the two-stream instability. When the Alfven waves reach the radius where electron-positron pairs are insufficient to supply the required current, a strong electric field forms accelerating the particle bunches along the field. The coherent FRB radiation is then produced. This model has been applied to FRB 20200428, which is associated with a magnetar (Lu et al., 2020; Wang, 2020)

### Cosmic comb model

The cosmic comb is a model taking two-body interaction into consideration to produce FRBs (Zhang, 2017). In this model, an NS may produce a bright FRB if its magnetosphere is combed by a close, strong plasma stream toward the anti-stream direction. The radiation is detectable only in the inferior conjunction configuration. The plasma stream could be an outflow from an SMBH. In this model, the properties of FRBs are determined by the stream energy flux received by the NS (Zhang, 2017). Due to the need for the NS magnetic field to be modified, the ram pressure of the stream is required to be greater than the magnetic pressure of the NS magnetosphere. For a non-relativistic and cold flow, the condition can be expressed as

P_{\\rm ram}>\\frac{B^{2}}{8\\pi}. If a dipolar magnetic configuration of the NS is assumed, the magnetic pressure is

\\frac{B_{\\rm LC}^{2}}{8\\pi}=\\frac{B_{s}^{2}}{8\\pi}\\left(\\frac{\\Omega R_{s}}{c} \\right)^{6}\\simeq 3.4~{}{\\rm erg~{}cm}^{-3}B_{s,12}^{2}P^{-6},  where B_{\\rm LC} is the magnetic field strength at the light cylinder, B_{s} and R_{s} are the surface magnetic field strength and radius of NS, and \\Omega and P are the angular frequency and rotation period of the NS, respectively. If the above condition is satisfied, the plasma stream arrives and significantly modifies the surface magnetic field of the NS toward the anti-stream direction. After that, magnetic reconnections may be triggered, releasing considerable energy that is adequate to accelerate the particles moving along the magnetic field at relativistic speeds within a short timescale. Eventually, an FRB can be produced through the coherent curvature radiation mechanism.

## 3 Emri Signals

We consider a system in which an NS moves along a highly eccentric orbit around the central SMBH, and the orbital pericenter decays owing to the GW radiation, producing the EMRI signals that may be detected by LISA and Tianqin. In such a system, except for the EMRI signals, there are two different ways to produce an FRB. Firstly, the outflow from the SMBH may comb the magnetosphere of NS, producing a bright FRB according to the cosmic comb model. Secondly, an FRB can be produced in the magnetosphere of NS according to the model of Kumar and Bosnjak (2020).

### Constraints on orbit

It is worth noting that only the scenario an FRB is generated through the cosmic comb model sets limits on the orbit of the binary system. The scenario in which an FRB is produced directly from the magnetosphere without interacting with the SMBH outflow places no restrictions on the orbit. The following discussion in this subsection is only for the cosmic comb scenario.

The mass loss rate of an SMBH can be expressed in the form of Eddington accretion rate \\dot{M}_{\\rm Edd} with a dimensionless parameter f reads (Yang et al., 2023)

\\dot{M}=f\\dot{M}_{\\rm Edd}=\\frac{4\\pi Gm_{\\rm p}}{\\epsilon_{\\rm BH}\\sigma_{T}c }fM_{\\rm BH}\\simeq 2.2\\times 10^{-3}~{}{\\rm M_{\\odot}yr}^{-1}f\\left(\\frac{M_{ \\rm BH}}{10^{5}{\\rm M_{\\odot}}}\\right). 

We consider an outflow from the SMBH with a typical dimensionless velocity \\beta=v/c=\\beta_{-2}/10^{2}. The ram pressure of the stream at the semi-major axis of orbit a is given by (Zhang, 2017)

P_{\\rm ram}\\simeq 1.6~{}{\\rm erg~{}cm}^{-3}\\left(\\frac{\\dot{M}}{M_{\\odot}{\\rm yr }^{-1}}\\right)\\beta_{-2}\\left(\\frac{a}{10^{-2}{\\rm pc}}\\right)^{-2}. 

Substituting Equation (2) and Equation (4) into Equation (1), we find that the ram pressure can be modified as

B_{\\rm s,13}^{2}P^{-6}\\leq 0.46\\left(\\frac{\\dot{M}}{M_{\\odot}{\\rm yr}^{-1}} \\right)\\beta_{-2}\\left(\\frac{a}{10^{-2}{\\rm pc}}\\right)^{-2}. 

When the typical parameters of NS (i.e. B_{s},P) are given, the comb condition P_{\\rm ram}>P_{\\rm B,LC} can be used to constrain the semi-axis a

\\frac{a}{10^{-2}{\\rm pc}}\\leq\\left(1.012\\times 10^{-3}\\left(\\frac{M_{\\rm BH}}{10 ^{5}~{}M_{\\odot}}\\right)f\\beta_{-2}B_{\\rm s,13}^{-2}P^{6}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{2}}. 

For the RM contribution, it can be estimated as (Yang et al., 2023)

{\\rm RM}\\sim\\frac{e^{3}}{2\\pi m_{e}^{2}c^{4}}B_{r}n_{\\rm e}a\\simeq 1.6\\times 10^ {5}~{}{\\rm rad~{}m}^{-2}f\\times\\left(\\frac{M_{\\rm BH}}{10^{5}~{}M_{\\odot}} \\right)\\left(\\frac{\\beta_{-2}}{1}\\right)^{-1}\\left(\\frac{B_{r}}{1~{}{\\rm mG} }\\right)\\left(\\frac{a}{10^{-2}~{}{\\rm pc}}\\right)^{-1},  where B_{r} is the magnetic field of SMBH at r\\sim a. We take Equation (6) as the upper limit of the separation between the NS and the SMBH, and fix \\beta_{-2} to 1. We consider the distances of NSs to be far from the gravitational radius of SMBHs. Therefore, relativistic corrections do not need to be considered here. Combing with Equation (7), we can estimate the lower limit of RM contributed by the SMBH outflow as

{\\rm RM}_{\\rm lowlim}=5.03\\times 10^{3}~{}{\\rm rad~{}m}^{-2}B_{\\rm s,13}P^{-3} \\left(\\frac{f}{10^{-6}}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\left(\\frac{M_{\\rm BH}}{10^{5}M_ {\\odot}}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\left(\\frac{B_{r}}{1{\\rm mG}}\\right). The derived RM and DM should be consistent with the observations of those FRBs that may be in the vicinities of SMBHs. This kind of FRB has relatively large RMs and DMs. According to current observations, only FRB 20121102 (Michilli et al., 2018; Zhang, 2018) and FRB 20190520B (Dai et al., 2022; Anna-Thomas et al., 2023) could satisfy this criterion. So we set the accessible range below

1.6\\times 10^{4}f\\left(\\frac{M_{\\rm BH}}{10^{5}M_{\\odot}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{a}{1 0^{-2}{\\rm pc}}\\right)^{-1}\\geq{\\rm RM}_{\\rm lowlim}, 

{\\rm DM}\\sim 2f\\left(\\frac{M_{\\rm BH}}{10^{5}M_{\\odot}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{a}{1 pc}\\right)^{-1}\\sim\\left(10-10^{3}\\right). 

DM is estimated from the Equation (49) in Yang et al. (2023). Besides, in a binary system containing an SMBH, the semi-major axis a is supposed to satisfy the condition that the pericenter p, which can be expressed as p=a\\left(1-e\\right), should be much larger than the gravitational radius r_{g} to prevent the companion star from falling into the horizon of the SMBH

a(1-e)\\gg r_{g}=\\frac{2GM_{\\rm BH}}{c^{2}}. 

EMRI begins with the scattering capture of NS by SMBH, this process tends to result in a high initial eccentricity orbit, which is expected to maintain the GW radiation in the LISA band (Gair et al., 2017). So we take a higher eccentricity range, 0.5\\leq e\\leq 1. Considering reasonable values of e, we can roughly derive

a\\gg\\frac{4GM_{\\rm BH}}{c^{2}}. 

Once the value of f is determined, the upper limit value of a can be simply derived by using Equation (6). Assuming the outflow of the low-luminosity SMBHs have similar properties with the Sgr A* (Ressler et al., 2023), we set a range of dimensionless parameter f characterizing mass loss rate from 10^{-7} to 10^{-2}(Wada et al., 2021). Here we will take a narrower range because a large value of f (i.e. typically greater than 10^{-4}) corresponds to an overlarge {\\rm RM}_{\\rm lowlim}, which has never appeared in previous observations. The possible range of the dimensionless parameter f can be estimated as

f\\sim\\left(1\\times 10^{-7}-1\\times 10^{-4}\\right). 

However, f=10^{-7} corresponds to an extremely short merge time (e.g. \\leq 1yr). Hence, we set the value of f from 10^{-6} to 10^{-4} which is a much more reasonable range.

### S/N of EMRIs

The strength of EMRI is determined by the strain amplitude h_{0}. The low-frequency signals in the LISA and Tianqin band will last for many years, which means that the n^{th} harmonic of the binary will spend approximately f_{n}^{2}/\\dot{f}_{n} cycles near the fundamental frequency f_{n}.

To account for the integration of the accumulated signal, the characteristic strain amplitude is used to represent the amplitude detected by the detector over the entire mission time (Finn and Thorne, 2000)

h_{c,n}^{2}=\\left(\\frac{f_{n}^{2}}{\\dot{f}_{n}}\\right)h_{n}^{2}. 

This formula is used to derive the GW strain amplitude at each instantaneous moment

h_{c,n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\pi D_{L}\\right)^{2}}\\frac{2G}{c^{3}}\\frac{\\dot{E}_ {n}}{\\dot{f}_{n}},  where D_{\\rm L} is the luminosity distance, \\dot{E}_{\\rm n} is the power of the n^{th} harmonic, \\dot{f}_{n}=-2\\dot{P}/P^{2} is the chirp rate, and P is the period of the orbit. The S/N can be estimated by (Robson et al., 2019)

({\\rm S/N})^{2}=\\int_{f_{GW}}^{f_{0W2}}\\frac{h_{c}^{2}(f_{GW})}{h_{n}^{2}(f_{ GW})}d(\\ln f_{GW}),  where h_{n}^{2}=f_{GW}S_{n}(f_{GW}) is the spectral density of the detector. The S/N also can be expressed as

({\\rm S/N})^{2}=\\frac{h_{c}^{2}\\Delta f_{GW}}{\\dot{f}_{GW}^{2}S_{n}(f_{GW})}. The effective strain is defined as (Barack & Cutler, 2004)

h_{\\rm eff}=h_{\\rm c}\\sqrt{\\Delta f_{GW}/f_{GW}}. 

The h_{\\rm eff} can be expressed by h_{n}(Chen et al., 2022)

h_{\\rm eff}=h_{n}\\sqrt{t_{\\rm LISA}f_{GW}}=h_{n}\\sqrt{N_{\\rm obs}},  where N_{\\rm obs} is the number of GW cycles during the whole observing time of LISA, the more cycles that LISA observes the higher the signal-noise ratio will be. We assume the 4 yr mission time of LISA (Amaro-Seoane et al., 2023). For incoherent search and coherent search of LISA, the threshold of S/N is 20 and 12-14 respectively. Here we set the threshold of the coherent search S/N to be 12. For Tianqin, the threshold of S/N is 10 (Moroianu et al., 2023).

#### 3.2.1 Scenario 1: FRBs produced by cosmic comb model

For this scenario, FRB is produced by the interaction between the NS and the outflow from SMBH. Therefore, the NS orbit should satisfy the cosmic comb conditions discussed in Section 2.2 and Section 3.1. Considering a harsh condition to produce FRBs, we assume the companion star is a magnetar. The surface magnetic field strength of magnetar B_{\\rm s} is fixed to 10^{14} G and the period P is fixed to 2 s, which are in the ranges derived from observations (Kaneko et al., 2010; Esposito et al., 2010; Kargaltsev et al., 2012; Kaspi & Beloborodov, 2017). According to observations, we have little information about B_{\\rm r} near SMBHs. It has been estimated to be about 1 mG at 0.12 pc from Sgr A* (Eatough et al., 2013). So we choose B_{\\rm r}\\sim 1 mG for the 10^{6}M_{\\odot} SMBH. We assume the spatial scale l\\propto r_{g}, which is related to the inner disk radius with the innermost stable circular orbit. The density of matters in the outflow from SMBH can be estimated as (Porth & Fendt, 2010)

\\rho=\\frac{m}{V}\\sim\\frac{\\dot{M}t}{\\beta}=\\frac{f\\dot{M}_{\\rm Edd}\\frac{l}{v} }{l^{3}}=\\alpha\\frac{f}{M_{\\rm BH}},  where V is the typical spatial volume, t is the typical time scale, and \\alpha is the normalized constant. The magnetic field in the outflow is

B_{\\rm r}=v_{\\rm A}(4\\pi\\rho)^{1/2},  where v_{\\rm A} is the Alfven velocity. Eventually, we have the relationship between magnetic field strength and mass of SMBH, i.e., B_{\\rm r}\\propto f^{0.5}M_{\\rm BH}^{-0.5}. We adopt this scaling relationship in our calculations. The results are shown in Table 1 for different cases with different selected parameter values. As discussed before, we fix the mass range of SMBH to (10^{5}M_{\\odot}-10^{7}M_{\\odot}). The range of dimensionless parameter of SMBH mass loss rate f is fixed to (10^{-6}-10^{-4}). We can infer that the maximal horizon of this event is z\\sim 0.04 for the optimistic case. The horizon of detection can be larger if a smaller value of a is reached, but such a system can not exist for enough time to be detected.

In the above calculations, due to the evolution timescales being much shorter than the observation time, the orbits are considered to be static. Nevertheless, the orbits of the top two cases (i.e. cases 1 and 2) in Table 1 only last for less than 62 yr. The evolution of orbits may need to be considered. We show the results in Figure 2. The GWs may be detected at a further distance (i.e. z\\sim 0.2) or at different frequencies because the orbital parameters are changing with time if the orbits are considered to be evolving.

